<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saxony</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saxony is a name that has moved across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It first meant the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> in the north-west, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>, conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> and later ruled as the Duchy of Saxony. After that duchy was broken up in 1180, the name and the electoral title passed to lands on the middle Elbe, held from 1423 by the Wettins, so that today's Free State of Saxony, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, lies in the east.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saxony.jpg" alt="Saxony" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Madden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Saxony.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Saxony</b> was then governed by Frankish counts and bishops, and Christianity was spread through new sees such as Paderborn, Bremen and Hildesheim.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Saxony</b>’s Erzgebirge silver was struck from about 1500 into some of the first large silver coins of the type later called the thaler, the name that lives on in the word dollar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Hamburg takes its name from the Hammaburg, a fortification built in the early ninth century near the meeting of the Alster and the Elbe, probably under Charlemagne or Louis the Pious, to secure the Frankish frontier in <b>Saxony</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Congress of Vienna, joined 39 sovereign states and free cities: the Austrian Empire and the Kingdom of Prussia for their former imperial lands, kingdoms such as Bavaria, <b>Saxony</b>, Württemberg and Hanover, dozens of grand duchies, duchies and principalities, and the free cities of Frankfurt, Hamburg, Bremen and Lübeck.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Prokop the Great, armies of townsmen and peasants used wagon forts of chained war wagons, defended with handguns and light artillery, to defeat the crusades launched by Emperor Sigismund and the papacy, notably at Vítkov Hill in 1420 and Domažlice in 1431, and raided <b>Saxony</b>, Silesia and Hungary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His son, Elector Frederick III, wanted a royal crown to match the elevation of the Elector of <b>Saxony</b> to the Polish throne and of William of Orange to the English one.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
